    "english": "Freud interpreted flying dreams as symbolic expressions of childhood physical sensations — such as changes in breathing and muscle relaxation — combined with repressed sexual and power drives. In 'The Interpretation of Dreams' (1900), he argued that aerial flight frequently represents wish fulfillment: the ego's desire to escape the constraints of daily life and the controlling superego. The sensation of being lifted from the ground was sometimes linked to sexual arousal, while simultaneously reflecting an unconscious wish for social dominance and authority. In the Freudian framework, flying dreams are thus understood as a composite expression of suppressed desire and ego liberation."
