      "english": "Freud classified exam dreams as one of the quintessential 'typical dreams.' He notably observed that exam-anxiety dreams almost never connect to exams the dreamer has actually failed — instead, they recall exams the dreamer feared but ultimately passed. Freud's interpretation was that these dreams serve an anxiety-relief function: the unconscious reminds the dreamer, 'Don't be afraid of tomorrow's challenge — remember how you feared that old exam and yet survived it.' Freud's own dream analysis also found that exam dreams could intertwine academic insecurity with unresolved oedipal rivalry toward a father figure."
